                <!-- What is IRM -->
                <section class="content-section" id="what-is-irm">
                    <h2>What is IRM?</h2>
                    <p>
                        <strong>ServiceNow Integrated Risk Management (IRM)</strong> is a unified platform built on the 
                        Now Platform that connects risk and compliance activities across the entire enterprise. Instead of 
                        managing risk in spreadsheets and siloed tools, IRM centralizes everything — policies, controls, 
                        risk registers, audits, and regulatory tracking — into a single source of truth.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The core value proposition is bridging the gap between <strong>technical operations</strong> 
                        (IT, cybersecurity, cloud) and <strong>business strategy</strong> (executive risk decisions, 
                        compliance reporting, regulatory adherence). This means risk data flows directly from operational 
                        systems like the CMDB and SecOps into dashboards that leadership can act on.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--important">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Key Concept</div>
                        <p>
                            IRM is not a standalone product — it's a suite of applications (Policy, Risk, Audit, Regulatory Change) 
                            that share a common data foundation on the Now Platform. This shared foundation is what makes 
                            cross-functional risk management possible.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- GRC to IRM -->
                <section class="content-section" id="grc-to-irm">
                    <h2>GRC → IRM Evolution</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow originally called this product <strong>GRC (Governance, Risk, and Compliance)</strong>. 
                        The rebranding to IRM reflects a broader industry shift:
                    </p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>GRC was reactive</strong> — focused on checking compliance boxes and passing audits</li>
                        <li><strong>IRM is proactive</strong> — focuses on continuous monitoring, risk-informed decisions, and connecting risk to business outcomes</li>
                        <li><strong>Operational Resilience</strong> — IRM adds capabilities for business continuity and operational resilience beyond traditional GRC</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>
                        Many table names still use the <code>sn_grc</code> prefix, which you'll encounter frequently as a developer. 
                        This is important context — when you see <code>sn_grc_*</code> tables, they are IRM tables.
                    </p>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--tip">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Developer Tip</div>
                        <p>
                            The table prefix <code>sn_grc</code> is retained for backward compatibility. Newer sub-modules 
                            may use <code>sn_compliance</code>, <code>sn_risk</code>, or <code>sn_audit</code> prefixes. 
                            Always check the application scope to understand which module owns a table.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Architecture -->
                <section class="content-section" id="architecture">
                    <h2>Architecture Layers</h2>
                    <p>
                        IRM's architecture can be understood as three distinct layers, all built on top of 
                        the Now Platform's core capabilities (workflow engine, CMDB, reporting, AI).
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">IRM Architecture Stack</div>
                        <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 12px; align-items: center;">
                            <div class="flow-step" style="width: 90%; max-width: 500px; background: rgba(99, 102, 241, 0.12); border-color: var(--accent-primary);">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Presentation Layer</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc" style="max-width: 400px;">Risk Workspace, Dashboards, Compliance Workspace, Audit Workspace</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">↕</span>
                            <div class="flow-step" style="width: 90%; max-width: 500px; background: rgba(139, 92, 246, 0.08); border-color: var(--accent-secondary);">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Application Layer</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc" style="max-width: 400px;">Risk Mgmt · Policy & Compliance · Audit · Regulatory Change · Operational Resilience</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">↕</span>
                            <div class="flow-step" style="width: 90%; max-width: 500px;">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Data & Integration Layer</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc" style="max-width: 400px;">Entity Framework · CMDB Integration · CSDM · M2M Relationships · Table API</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">↕</span>
                            <div class="flow-step" style="width: 90%; max-width: 500px; background: rgba(100, 116, 139, 0.08);">
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Now Platform Foundation</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc" style="max-width: 400px;">Workflow Engine · AI/ML · Reporting · Security · IntegrationHub</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <h3>Layer 1: Data & Integration</h3>
                    <p>
                        At the foundation sits the <strong>Entity Framework</strong> and the structured data model. 
                        Entities (things being monitored — servers, departments, processes) are linked to risks, 
                        controls, and policies through configuration-based relationships. The CMDB provides the 
                        source of truth for IT assets, and the Common Service Data Model (CSDM) aligns service 
                        architecture with risk data.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Layer 2: Application</h3>
                    <p>
                        The core IRM applications (Risk, Policy & Compliance, Audit, Regulatory Change) operate 
                        on this shared data foundation. Each application has its own workflow logic, state models, 
                        and automation — but they all read from and write to the same underlying tables. A failed 
                        control in Policy & Compliance can automatically generate a risk in Risk Management.
                    </p>

                    <h3>Layer 3: Presentation</h3>
                    <p>
                        Persona-based Workspaces provide role-specific views. A Risk Manager sees the Risk Workspace 
                        with heatmaps and registers, while an Auditor sees the Audit Workspace with project timelines 
                        and evidence tracking. These are configurable through the Workspace Builder.
                    </p>
                </section>

                <!-- Core Modules -->
                <section class="content-section" id="core-modules">
                    <h2>Core Modules</h2>
                    <p>
                        The IRM suite consists of several tightly integrated applications. Here's what each one does:
                    </p>

                    <div class="tabs-container">
                        <div class="tabs-header">
                            <button class="tab-btn active" data-tab="tab-risk">Risk Management</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-policy">Policy & Compliance</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-audit">Audit Management</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-rcm">Regulatory Change</button>
                            <button class="tab-btn" data-tab="tab-resilience">Operational Resilience</button>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel active" id="tab-risk">
                            <h3>Risk Management</h3>
                            <p>
                                Identifies, assesses, and treats risks across the organization. Provides a structured 
                                risk register where each risk goes through a defined lifecycle (Draft → Assess → Respond → Monitor → Retired).
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Risk Register:</strong> Central repository of all identified risks</li>
                                <li><strong>Advanced Risk Assessment (ARA):</strong> Configurable methodology for scoring inherent, residual, and target risk</li>
                                <li><strong>Risk Response:</strong> Mitigate, avoid, transfer, or accept strategies with approval workflows</li>
                                <li><strong>Risk Appetite:</strong> Define tolerance thresholds that trigger automatic responses</li>
                                <li><strong>RCSA:</strong> Risk & Control Self-Assessment projects for simultaneous evaluation</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-policy">
                            <h3>Policy & Compliance Management</h3>
                            <p>
                                Manages the entire lifecycle of internal policies, maps them to external regulations, 
                                and continuously monitors compliance through automated controls and indicators.
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Authority Documents:</strong> External regulations and standards (NIST, ISO, SOX, GDPR)</li>
                                <li><strong>Policies:</strong> Internal organizational policies derived from authority documents</li>
                                <li><strong>Controls:</strong> Specific implementation of policy requirements</li>
                                <li><strong>Indicators:</strong> Automated or manual tests that verify control effectiveness (Pass/Fail)</li>
                                <li><strong>Issues & Remediation:</strong> Tracked when controls fail or gaps are identified</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-audit">
                            <h3>Audit Management</h3>
                            <p>
                                Automates internal audit processes from planning through execution to reporting, 
                                with full evidence collection and stakeholder collaboration.
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Audit Planning:</strong> Define audit scope, objectives, and resource allocation</li>
                                <li><strong>Test Management:</strong> Execute control tests with configurable test plans and templates</li>
                                <li><strong>Evidence Collection:</strong> Automated evidence requests with attachment support</li>
                                <li><strong>Findings & Recommendations:</strong> Track audit findings and link to remediation tasks</li>
                                <li><strong>Audit Reports:</strong> Generate comprehensive reports with dashboards</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-rcm">
                            <h3>Regulatory Change Management</h3>
                            <p>
                                Tracks upcoming regulatory changes and assesses their impact on the organization's 
                                compliance posture. Critical for heavily regulated industries.
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Regulatory Alerts:</strong> Centralized feed of regulatory changes from intelligence sources</li>
                                <li><strong>Impact Assessments:</strong> Guided playbooks to evaluate how changes affect business units</li>
                                <li><strong>Taxonomy Mapping:</strong> Map multiple provider taxonomies to your internal taxonomy</li>
                                <li><strong>Change Campaigns:</strong> Multi-task regulatory updates across the organization</li>
                                <li><strong>AI Compliance Mapping:</strong> ML-powered mapping of regulations to internal citations</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>

                        <div class="tab-panel" id="tab-resilience">
                            <h3>Operational Resilience</h3>
                            <p>
                                Extends IRM beyond traditional GRC to cover business continuity, operational 
                                resilience planning, and crisis response management.
                            </p>
                            <ul>
                                <li><strong>Business Continuity:</strong> Plan and test recovery strategies for critical services</li>
                                <li><strong>Impact Tolerance:</strong> Define maximum tolerable disruption levels for key services</li>
                                <li><strong>Scenario Testing:</strong> Model "what-if" scenarios to assess organizational preparedness</li>
                                <li><strong>Case Management:</strong> Handle risk events, exceptions, and remediation workflows</li>
                            </ul>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Implementation Phases -->
                <section class="content-section" id="implementation">
                    <h2>Implementation Phases</h2>
                    <p>
                        ServiceNow recommends a phased approach to IRM adoption, often described as 
                        <strong>Foundational → Crawl → Walk → Run → Fly</strong>.
                    </p>

                    <div class="diagram-block">
                        <div class="diagram-title">IRM Maturity Phases</div>
                        <div class="flow-container">
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">01</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Foundation</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Design entity model & data architecture</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">02</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Crawl</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Basic policy & risk management</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">03</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Walk</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Automated controls & indicators</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">04</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Run</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">Cross-module integration & RCSA</span>
                            </div>
                            <span class="flow-arrow">→</span>
                            <div class="flow-step">
                                <span class="flow-step-num">05</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-label">Fly</span>
                                <span class="flow-step-desc">AI-driven, continuous monitoring</span>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <div class="info-card info-card--warning">
                        <div class="info-card-title">Implementation Tip</div>
                        <p>
                            The most critical phase is <strong>Foundation</strong>. Getting the entity model and data 
                            architecture right from the start is essential. Rushing to build controls and indicators 
                            without a solid entity framework will lead to rework and data quality issues down the line.
                        </p>
                    </div>
                </section>

                <!-- Lifecycle Diagram -->
                <section class="content-section" id="lifecycle-diagram">
                    <h2>End-to-End Lifecycle Diagram</h2>
                    <p>
                        This flowchart illustrates the end-to-end lifecycle of how external regulations are 
                        translated into internal controls, how those controls are scoped to entities, and how 
                        failures are managed through issues and risks.
                    </p>
                    <div class="mermaid" >
                        graph TD
                            %% Define classes for styling
                            classDef external fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                            classDef policy fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#34d399,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                            classDef entity fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#10b981,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                            classDef verify fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#22c55e,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                            classDef risk fill:#1e1e2f,stroke:#ef4444,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff
                            
                            subgraph "1. Compliance Foundation"
                            A[Authority Document<br/>e.g., ISO 27001]:::external --> B[Citations<br/>Specific Clauses]:::external
                            B --> C[Policy<br/>Internal Rule]:::policy
                            C --> D[Control Objective<br/>Policy Statement]:::policy
                            end
                            
                            subgraph "2. Entity & Scoping"
                            E[Entity Type<br/>e.g., Windows Servers]:::entity -->|Generates| F[Entity<br/>e.g., Server Prod-01]:::entity
                            F -.->|Relates to| G[CMDB CI]:::entity
                            end
                            
                            subgraph "3. Operationalization"
                            D -->|Instantiated for Entity| H[Control<br/>Specific Implementation]:::verify
                            F -->|Assigned to| H
                            end
                            
                            subgraph "4. Continuous Monitoring"
                            H --> I[Indicator<br/>Automated Test]:::verify
                            H --> J[Attestation<br/>Manual Check]:::verify
                            
                            I -->|Pass| K((Compliant)):::verify
                            J -->|Pass| K
                            
                            I -->|Fail| L[Issue<br/>Compliance Gap]:::risk
                            J -->|Fail| L
                            end
                            
                            subgraph "5. Remediation & Risk"
                            L --> M[Remediation Task]:::risk
                            L -->|Escalates to| N[Risk Assessment<br/>ARA / RAM]:::risk
                            N --> O{Risk Response}:::risk
                            O -->|Mitigate| M
                            O -->|Accept| P[Risk Acceptance]:::risk
                            end

                            click A "/irm/audit-regulatory#what-is-audit"
                            click B "/irm/audit-regulatory#what-is-audit"
                            click C "/irm/policy-compliance#hierarchy"
                            click D "/irm/policy-compliance#hierarchy"
                            click E "/irm/data-model"
                            click F "/irm/data-model"
                            click G "/irm/data-model"
                            click H "/irm/policy-compliance#controls"
                            click I "/irm/policy-compliance#indicators"
                            click J "/irm/policy-compliance#hierarchy"
                            click L "/irm/policy-compliance#issues"
                            click M "/irm/policy-compliance#issues"
                            click N "/irm/risk-lifecycle"
                            click O "/irm/risk-lifecycle"
                            click P "/irm/risk-lifecycle"
                    </div>
                </section>

